    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2591f08250f67210b019c6984899691ed58019dd4eb25edca10806bf8bceeb85",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The full Voronoi expansion supplies the oscillatory structure but only bounded convergence on each fixed finite interval, which is insufficient for termwise integration over an improper range. The proof repairs that gap by cutting the integral first, substituting the expansion on the finite interval, invoking bounded convergence to interchange sum and integral there, and applying a finite-cut saddle-point lemma. The resulting terms are then separated so the sole outer-cut dependence and the remaining errors can be estimated individually; only after absolute convergence and negligible bounds are established is the cut sent to infinity and the components assembled.",
