    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_72d445d8925d7037347a6d297b41802d79f152e62f831f9b75caad732e5af7a4",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The proof keeps u and v independent while establishing and continuing two representations, and equates them only after the auxiliary function g has been continued into a region where the representations can overlap. It then specializes to the coupled slice used by the mean-square problem, integrates in height, and removes routine gamma-factor behavior with Stirling asymptotics, leaving g on that slice as the remaining analytic burden. Decomposing g into four components makes that burden inspectable: two pieces are routed through absolute convergence, a third through an alternative continuation formula, and the fourth through a convergence estimate using the truncated divisor bound; only after those steps does Fubini reorganize the result into components for saddle analysis.",
